# Security headers for the frontend, served by Cloudflare Workers' [assets]
# binding (see worker/wrangler.toml).
#
# WHY THIS FILE AND NOT worker.js: with [assets], a request that matches a
# static file is served by the platform without ever invoking the Worker's
# fetch handler, so headers set in worker.js never reach these responses.
# This is the same precedence that made landing/functions/setup.js's CSP dead
# code for months — see the comment at the top of that file.
#
# ⚠ KEEP IN SYNC with the CSP in landing/functions/_lib/bundle-worker.js.
# That file is how EVERY wizard-deployed install serves its frontend (inlined
# into the Worker, not via [assets]), so it needs the same policy applied a
# completely different way. Changing one without the other means Hans's own
# instance and every customer's install disagree about their own security.
#
# TWO POLICIES, on purpose:
#
#   The app (/ and /index.html) holds all memory content and is where any XSS
#   would actually matter. It has zero inline <script> blocks, zero inline
#   event handlers, and one external script, so it can take a strict
#   script-src — which is the directive that does the real work.
#
#   The docs pages (help*.html, support.html) carry 2-3 inline <script> blocks
#   each for back-button and language-switch logic. They render no user data
#   at all, so 'unsafe-inline' there costs nothing an attacker could use.
#   Extracting those scripts to files would let both pages share one strict
#   policy — worth doing eventually, not worth refactoring five stable pages
#   for right now.
#
# Directive notes:
#   style-src needs 'unsafe-inline' because index.html uses 35 inline
#     style="..." attributes. CSS-in-JS via el.style.x is NOT affected by CSP.
#   img-src needs data: (attachment previews are data:image base64) and blob:
#     (photo thumbnails and the lightbox use URL.createObjectURL).
#   media-src needs blob: for TTS audio playback.
#   worker-src 'self' is what lets sw.js register at all. Without it the
#     directive falls back to default-src ('none') and registration fails
#     outright — which would silently remove the in-app install prompt,
#     since Chromium only offers it when a service worker is registered.
#     The docs pages register nothing and deliberately don't get it.
#   connect-src 'self' assumes the frontend and Worker share an origin, which
#     is true for every wizard install and for wrangler deploy. An install
#     that points the app at a DIFFERENT worker URL would break here.

# ⚠ Cloudflare applies EVERY matching rule, not just the most specific one, and
# a browser given two CSP headers enforces their INTERSECTION. So a broad /*
# CSP would silently re-impose the strict script-src on the docs pages and
# break them. CSP is therefore declared per-document and never via /*.
# Non-document assets (app.js, style.css, images) need no CSP at all — the
# header only means anything in a document context.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: same-origin

/
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/index.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/help*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

/support.html
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
